lated peptide  so if you click here this spectrum is  annotated  and then above it we can zoom and we can  see with a ruler of here's where the  glycan is  found between two of the fragments  let me uh  go to the next slide and show you  basically how this is working  so you can take your mouse and you can  click on the different dots and that  will show the spectrum so in this case  i'm going to start from the immunoside  one residue before the n glycan  and so you can see the c7 ion the c8  the c9  coming from the other side you can see  the spectrum  as you walk in uh so you're getting uh  identifications with fragments on both  sides  of the chain so you have a very high  degree of confidence of what this is  there's also a leucine uh present four  amino acids in from the carboxy terminus  and this is showing the spectrum that's  seen here so in this particular case  the side chain fragment is actually much  more dominant than the  main chain fragment the c4  and that has to do with the ions being  activated and the side chain  fragmentation coming apart  very quickly after the electron capture  event  so we're getting exceptional coverage of  side chain fragmentations that really  helps with identification  it's also very useful with side chain  fragmentation to note that  that breaks cysteine bonds  and here's an example of analyzing  insulin so this is  uh  insulin that's not reduced and it's  eluting off a standard hplc column  and you can see that you get very nice  fragments with ecd spectrum that are  nicely resolved  and if we start to analyze that what we  found was we could characterize where  all three disulfide bonds were located  and to illustrate this  we have to analyze it in six runs but  basically we compare we consider  breaking this bond  we look for the b chain  and we consider the a chain as a  post-translational modification  and we can get extensive fragmentation  across the b chain  repeat the analysis and you look for the  a chain with t